1
00:00:00,476 --> 00:00:03,576
1973, UC Berkeley: a program called SPICE learns to simulate
circuits.

2
00:00:03,777 --> 00:00:07,042
Tonight that 1973 program wakes up, inside your browser.

3
00:00:07,242 --> 00:00:08,723
Circuit Séance: a hand-drawn circuit is read into a SPICE
netlist…

4
00:00:08,923 --> 00:00:11,391
…and ngspice, Berkeley SPICE’s open-source heir compiled to
WebAssembly, simulates it on your device.

5
00:00:11,591 --> 00:00:13,992
No server, no install, no account. The demo makes zero network
requests.

6
00:00:14,192 --> 00:00:16,745
Hover the green-phosphor scope to read any instant…

7
00:00:16,945 --> 00:00:24,624
…and the Spirit explains the trace with numbers measured from
the simulation: τ = 1 ms = R × C.

8
00:00:24,824 --> 00:00:26,935
New: possess a component. Pick C1 and its spirit takes the
slider.

9
00:00:27,135 --> 00:00:33,056
Drag it: ngspice re-runs on every move, and the dashed ghost
is the circuit before possession.

10
00:00:33,256 --> 00:00:35,656
C × 2.2 → τ × 2.2. The readout shows it live: 1 ms becomes 2.2
ms.

11
00:00:35,856 --> 00:00:42,926
Now ÷10: the capacitor fills ten times faster.

12
00:00:43,126 --> 00:00:48,594
Homework check: solve it by hand, then let the simulation
grade you (±5 %).

13
00:00:48,794 --> 00:00:53,048
A classic slip, τ = RC/2, gets caught: off by −50 %.

14
00:00:53,248 --> 00:00:57,644
Fix it, check again: correct. A lab partner for every EE
problem set.

15
00:00:57,844 --> 00:00:59,836
Haunted Classics: famous circuits resurrected from old pages.

16
00:01:00,036 --> 00:01:05,941
The 1972 NE555 timer: its capacitor saws between ⅓ and ⅔ of
the supply.

17
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:13,761
Bill Hewlett’s 1939 Wien-bridge oscillator: a sine wave grows
out of a 10 mV whisper.

18
00:01:13,961 --> 00:01:22,761
Every label and every explanation also works in Chinese, fully
offline, with no blocked services.

19
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:28,556
Bring your own key (Qwen-VL by default) to read photos of your
own drawings.

20
00:01:28,756 --> 00:01:32,442
The key stays in this browser and goes only to the provider
you pick. The demo needs none.

21
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:38,215
We built it spec-first with Kiro: steering, an EARS
requirements spec, a design and a linked task list.

22
00:01:38,415 --> 00:01:43,025
Every requirement is a WHEN … THE SYSTEM SHALL line that tasks
link back to.

23
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:47,433
Kiro implemented the tasks in headless waves and ticked them
off.

24
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:52,645
Agent hooks fired on every save: lint, netlist tests, and a
spirit-tone review that hunts for leaked keys.

25
00:01:52,845 --> 00:01:56,646
When the free credits ran out, we finished by hand, and logged
every hand-written file.

26
00:02:02,598 --> 00:02:08,599
Circuit Séance · 电路招魂. Berkeley SPICE, resurrected in your
browser.
